<article class="env-lemma" id="0GSA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GSA">Lemma <span data-tag="0GSA">37.37.3</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be morphisms of schemes. Let $x \in X$ with image $y \in Y$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$Y$ is integral and geometrically unibranch at $y$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$g \circ f$ is étale at $x$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there is a specialization $x' \leadsto x$ such that $f(x')$ is the generic point of $Y$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $f$ is étale at $x$ and $g$ is étale at $y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      After replacing $X$ by an open neighbourhood of $x$ we may assume $g \circ f$ is étale. Then we find $f$ is unramified by Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/02GG" data-tag="02GG">29.36.16</a> and <a href="/tag/02GK" data-tag="02GK">29.37.5</a>. Hence $f$ is étale at $x$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0GS8" data-tag="0GS8">37.37.1</a>. Then by étale descent we see that $g$ is étale at $y$, see for example Descent, Lemma <a href="/tag/02KM" data-tag="02KM">35.14.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>